# Day 1 — Project Setup & Initial Dataset

## Objective

The goal of this project is to build an end-to-end data engineering pipeline using Databricks, PySpark, SQL, Delta Lake, and GitHub.

I will start with a small dataset and gradually increase the data volume and complexity as the project progresses.

## What I Did Today

* Created a Databricks notebook for generating sample customer data.
* Generated an initial dataset of 20 customer records.
* Used Python/PySpark to create the data.
* Successfully ran the notebook in Databricks.
* Connected my Databricks repository to GitHub.
* Committed and pushed my initial notebook to GitHub.
* Created a project notes file to document my learning and progress.

## Initial Dataset

The dataset contains customer information such as:

* Customer ID
* Customer name
* Country
* Email

I started with only 20 records so I can understand each stage of the data pipeline before scaling the project.

## What I Learned

I learned that Databricks can be integrated with GitHub through a Git repository, allowing notebooks and project files to be version-controlled.

I also learned that Git commits can be used to track my progress as I build the project.

## Planned Lakehouse Architecture

The project will eventually follow this structure:

Source Data
↓
Bronze Layer — raw/ingested data
↓
Silver Layer — cleaned and transformed data
↓
Gold Layer — business-ready data

## GitHub Progress

My initial data-generation notebook has been committed and pushed to GitHub.

This will allow my project development history to be visible to recruiters.

## Next Step

The next step is to create the **Bronze layer** and ingest the initial customer data into the lakehouse.

I will then learn how to store and manage the raw data using Delta Lake before moving to the Silver and Gold layers.

## Reflection

Starting with 20 records gives me a simple dataset that I can use to understand the pipeline before increasing the volume.

As the project grows, I will continue documenting what I learn, the problems I encounter, and how I solve them.
